? Classification differs from (numeric) prediction because classification predicts:
+ Categorical class labels; prediction models continuous values
- Continuous values; prediction predicts categorical labels
- Clusters of similar objects, without any class labels
- Only yes/no answers, never more than two classes
= Both are supervised learning.

? Why must the test set be independent of the training set?
+ Testing on training data gives an over-optimistic accuracy (overfitting)
- It makes training faster because there is less data to learn from
- The algorithm cannot run unless the two sets are separate
- It reduces the number of attributes the model has to use
= An overfitted model learns anomalies of the training data.

? In a decision tree, a **leaf node** represents:
- A test on an attribute
- An outcome of a test
+ A class label
- The training set
= Internal nodes are tests; branches are outcomes.

? ID3, C4.5 and CART use which attribute selection measures?
+ Information gain, gain ratio, Gini index (respectively)
- Gini index, information gain, gain ratio (respectively)
- Support, confidence, lift (respectively)
- Gain ratio, Gini index, information gain (respectively)
= All three are greedy, top-down, recursive algorithms.

? A set has 9 "yes" and 5 "no". What is its entropy Info(D) (3 decimals)?
# 0.940 ~0.001
= −(9/14)log₂(9/14) − (5/14)log₂(5/14) = 0.940.

? Info(D) = 0.940 and Info_age(D) = 0.694. What is Gain(age)?
# 0.246 ~0.001
= Gain = 0.940 − 0.694 = 0.246, the highest, so age is the root.

? A node with all tuples in one class has entropy:
# 0
= A pure node has zero uncertainty.

? Why use **gain ratio** instead of information gain?
+ Information gain favours many-valued attributes (e.g. IDs); gain ratio corrects this
- Gain ratio is faster because it skips the whole entropy calculation for each split
- Information gain cannot handle numeric attributes at all
- Gain ratio does not need class labels to choose a split
= Gain ratio = Gain ÷ SplitInfo.

? Gini index for a node with class proportions 0.5 and 0.5 is:
# 0.5
= 1 − (0.25 + 0.25) = 0.5.

? With the Gini index (CART), the attribute chosen for the split is the one with:
+ The minimum Gini index (largest drop in impurity)
- The maximum Gini index (most mixed partitions)
- The most distinct values, giving the most branches
- The highest support among the candidate attributes
= There are 2ᵛ − 2 nonempty proper subsets, or (2ᵛ − 2)/2 distinct unordered binary splits.

? Which is **not** a criterion for comparing classifiers?
- Accuracy
- Speed
- Interpretability
+ Colour of the output
= The others are robustness and scalability.
